 fully 85  percent  of the na of the adult population that  still is only 72  percent protection so there's still  quite a sizable  percentage for the virus to go after and  infect  which is why we've got to be careful  hence why the four weeks of data  collection  and then one week of analysis and then  moving to the next step now the good  news is  we're not seeing any evidence that would  lead us to believe that we can't meet  the next step in may the really  important step 17th of may and then  ultimately  obviously the 21st of june but it's much  better to be careful  and follow the data collect the data  properly analyze it  and then make a decision then to  we all want to obviously get our  freedoms back as quickly as possible  but let's do this properties do it  safely well in terms of that  there's obviously now the talk about a  booster and a 60 million  fisa vaccines have been bought or  being bored where does that leave us in  terms of  of sort of cross-vaccination is that  going to be  fisa vaccines given to to people as a  booster whatever else they've taken in  the past  so for this current deployment so phase  one we've just completed the over 50s  uh everybody's been offered a first dose  of course we're now second dosings i  explained  that deployment continues so if you've  had pfizer you'll get a five second dose  you've had astrozenica you've got acidic  secondary moderna  moderna we have two clinical trials that  are  important one is the mix and match trial  which we've put  seven million pounds to fund that will  report at the  end of the summer that will help us for  future proofing  and planning of vaccination campaigns  the second one which jonathan van tan  referred to last night  which is the cove boost trial  which looks at which booster vaccine  single shot would be the best  now what we want to do and my focus at  the moment is to give the scientists  as many options as possible for that  future proofing whether it's pfizer  astrazeneca astrozenica variant  val neva the scottish manufactured  vaccine  and of course novovax which works  extremely well against both the current  dominant  virus which is the kent virus and the  south african virus i mean i spoke to  sir john bell  who's on the vaccine task force a couple  of weeks ago and  he was very much of the opinion looking  at the data he'd seen that actually  mixing the vaccines  would give a much stronger immune  response  it could do and which is why we've got  the mix and match uh clinical trials  they'll report at the end of the summer  the booster  trials as well the cove boost trials  will also be able to tell us  which booster shot would be the most  suitable and of course  the scientists will then have to decide  whether you  boost obviously the most if you recall  the most vulnerable uh  to covert we began vaccinating on the  8th of december  with pfizer and then 4th of january with  astrazeneca  so i want to give the scientists as many  choices of  vaccines to be able to use as a booster  they will decide whether  we go september or later in this year or  early next year depending on the  durability  of the current vaccination program  but we need to give them that ability to  have as many  different vaccines in their armory to be  able to continue  to manage the virus and i think we will  begin  by next year to hopefully move from  pandemic to endemic  and treat it like we would do with a flu  vaccination program and the other bit  obviously that the nhs  is planning at the moment is how do you  dovetail a  covert boost campaign with the flu  vaccination program in the autumn look i  can appreciate you're in a very  difficult position because i mean  your priority your focus is is  vaccinating  the united kingdom but when you're  looking at buying all these extra  vaccines so this is the 60  million doses of of pfizer when we don't  quite know if that's going to be the  right approach for everybody and you say  getting lots of these different vaccines  on board  well we know there is still this huge  global problem  at what point do we start to look at  this from westminster  as a global issue that we need to start  dealing with  really important question so literally  from the inception  of the vaccines task force when the  prime minister set up the vaccine starts  with  he gave us two priorities one find the  vaccines  that will work against this virus so go  out and look  and find them manufacture them in the uk  if you can  contract early if they're not  manufacturing the uk and get them here  but the second priority from back in  march of 2020  is how do you help the rest of the world  he said to have a plan of how we have  that so we're the first country to put  548 billion pounds to the kovacs which  is  the infrastructure that's now delivering  40 million vaccines of landed for over  100 countries so  philippines and ghana and ivory coast  uh have all been receiving kovacs the  bulk of it by the way is the oxford  astrozenica vaccine  the oxford team and astrazeneca are not  making any profit on that it's being  done at cost  whilst we're in pandemic around the  world  so we are doing a hell of a lot to make  sure we help the rest of  india has historically been there for  our nhs  many many colleagues who work in the nhs  are of  indian heritage it's really important  we're one of the  first countries to help in the hundreds  of uh pieces  of um health machinery that was needed  by them including oxygen concentrators  went out to india but we're also through  oxford and astrazeneca um giving  the serum institute the ability to  manufacture 70 million  doses a month for india and other  low-income countries  okay look you mentioned the prime  minister can we talk about his current  predicament  um sakia stammer said on television  last night with these investigations  inquiries that are underway  this could all be over in five minutes  he said if the prime minister just  tells us what went on with the refurb of  his flat why doesn't he do that  well first of all the prime minister has  answered  umpteen questions on this including as  your viewers would say  would want to know did the taxpayer pay  for the refurbishment  or not he paid for the refurbishment  unlike previous prime ministers tony  blair i think spent  some like 300 thousand pounds on  refurbishing uh  the flat in uh number ten over time  but here's the the problem for kirsten i  listened very carefully to him last  night  because he did a big sit-down interview  with robert peston 10 minutes  of that and pt barnum would have been  proud of him  he was the ultimate bandwagoner not a  single policy idea for an election  important elections coming up in seven  days time  all he could talk about yeah but come on  people was this question but people  could say the same about you saying well  you're trying to dodge  you're trying to dodge the issue by by  talking about their lack of policy  suggestions  we have elections we have elections in  seven days time but people want to know  what you're going to do are you going to  be  delivering a vaccine program are you  going to deliver a leveling up agenda  where we're investing  640 billion in infrastructure but the  prime minister has said as you said he  said repeatedly i  paid for this refurbishment well he  might have done in the end what we want  to know is where was the money coming  from originally the talk of  you know 58 000 coming from one donor  loans from cchq did any of that actually  happen  well first of all the uh prime minister  has  appointed lord guy who was the i'm  actually the queen's  um uh private secretary a crossbencher  in the house was  really important the reforms we've made  to the way uh lord  can effectively do his job is to allow  him to be proactive  in his investigation the prime minister  will absolutely  make sure that he delivers everything  that lord guide  asks for to be able to conduct that  investigation  the primers have been very clear that he  paid for this that he  also followed all the ministerial codes  took advice and if lord guyt in his  investigation  requires the prime minister to make any  other declarations then he will also  would that so that's a commitment is it  because ultimately as it stands the  prime minister  is the ultimate arbiter of this own  investigation  he could he could dismiss it he could  just dismiss the recommendations you  saying that  that whatever lord comes up with  independently  will be acted upon so this is a really  important question  part of our reforms now allow  lord guy to be able to be proactive  rather than reactive  all ministers serve with the confidence  of the prime minister  he's appointed lord guy to look at  ministers  and ministerial conduct it's only right  i serve because i have the confidence of  the prime minister if i don't have that  confidence  i will clearly lose my job so  ultimately the prime minister has done  everything in his power to answer these  questions  we've got elections in seven days time  labor at the moment  are a policy-free zone uh i worry  because  the voters will look at kirsten i think  they'll probably  be thinking well you know where where  are the ideas  where are you what are you going to do  to better my life how are you going to  give me a job at the end of this  pandemic are you going to  back uh this country being able to do  the vaccinations that it needs which  you know is important to make sure we  come out of this pandemic  okay as possible we will find out what  the voters think  in a week or so well really good to see  you this morning thank you very much  indeed

